# Gotchas

{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
## A sync driver silently blocks the whole event loop

This project's async session setup only works because the driver
underneath it is actually async: `sqlite+aiosqlite://` for SQLite,
`postgresql+asyncpg://` for PostgreSQL (see
`src/{{ package_name }}/core/config.py` and `.env`). If you ever change
a `database_url` to a sync driver — `sqlite://` (plain `sqlite3`) or
`postgresql+psycopg://`/`postgresql+psycopg2://` — `create_async_engine`
will raise immediately on startup (it validates the driver supports
`asyncio`), so this particular mistake doesn't linger silently.

The more dangerous version of this mistake is hand-rolling a database
call *outside* this project's session pattern — e.g. reaching for a
sync driver library directly, or calling a third-party client that
does blocking I/O, from inside an `async def` route handler. Unlike a
misconfigured engine, that won't raise: it will just block the entire
event loop for the duration of the call, stalling *every other
in-flight request*, not only the one that made the call. Low local
traffic hides this completely; it shows up as mysterious latency
spikes under real load. Stay inside `get_session()`/`AsyncSession` for
all database access and this can't happen.

## `expire_on_commit=False` is deliberate — don't "fix" it

`async_session_maker` is built with `expire_on_commit=False`. The
default (`True`) expires every attribute on a committed object, so the
next attribute access re-fetches from the database — but with an
*async* session, that re-fetch requires an `await`, and ordinary
attribute access (`item.name`) can't await anything. Route handlers
here return ORM instances right after `await session.commit()` (see
`create_item` in `routes/items.py`), so leaving the default in place
would break attribute access on the response the moment FastAPI tries
to serialize it. If you're debugging a `MissingGreenlet` error, this
setting (or the lack of it, in code that builds its own session
factory) is the first thing to check.

## Schema creation and migrations don't coexist

{% if migrations %}
This project has `migrations=true`: `main.py` deliberately does **not**
call `init_db()` at all (it's not even imported) — schema comes solely
from `alembic upgrade head`. `init_db()` in `core/db.py` still contains
a `create_all()` call, but nothing in the running app invokes it. If
you're seeing "table does not exist" errors, run the migration first
(see the `alembic` skill's `guides/first-migration.md`) — don't wire
`init_db()` back into `main.py`'s `lifespan` to "fix" it.
{% else %}
This project has `migrations=false`: `init_db()`'s `create_all()` runs
on every startup via the `lifespan` hook, so the schema always matches
the current models — convenient, but only correct because there's no
migration history to fall out of sync with. If you later turn
migrations on, this call is removed from `main.py`'s `lifespan`
entirely (see the `alembic` skill). `create_all()` and a real migration
history must never both run against the same database: if the tables
already exist by the time `alembic revision --autogenerate` runs
first, it permanently reports "No changes in schema detected" instead
of capturing the real schema.
{% endif %}

## Tests never call `init_db()`

`tests/conftest.py` builds its own `test_engine` pointed at
`sqlite+aiosqlite:///:memory:` and overrides the `get_session`
dependency directly — schema for it comes from an autouse fixture
calling `Base.metadata.create_all`/`drop_all` against that engine, not
from `init_db()`. This keeps the suite runnable with zero external
services regardless of what `DATABASE_URL` is configured to in `.env`.
See the `fastapi` skill's `guides/testing.md` for why `lifespan` (and
therefore `init_db()`) doesn't run during tests at all.
{% else %}
## `scoped_session` is thread-local — teardown is what keeps it safe

`SessionLocal = scoped_session(sessionmaker())` hands back the *same*
session object for every `SessionLocal()`/`get_session()` call on a
given thread, until something calls `SessionLocal.remove()`. `init_db()`
registers that removal as `app.teardown_appcontext(lambda exc=None:
SessionLocal.remove())` — if that registration is ever missing or
removed, sessions stop being cleaned up between requests: a WSGI worker
thread that handles request A then request B would hand request B
the *same* session object A used, still holding A's identity map,
possibly still holding A's uncommitted state or stale query results.
This is easy to miss in dev (one worker, low traffic, requests rarely
interleave in a way that exposes it) and shows up in production as data
from one request leaking into another, or `DetachedInstanceError`s that
only happen under concurrent load. Don't call `get_session()` (or
`SessionLocal()`) anywhere that isn't inside a request — a call at
import/module scope binds a session before `teardown_appcontext` exists
to clean it up.

## Sync driver mismatch still exists, just inverted

There's no async-vs-sync driver trap here the way there is for the
FastAPI/async setup — this project's sync engine expects a sync driver
by design, and both configured options are sync: plain `sqlite3` for
SQLite (`sqlite:///...`), `psycopg` (v3) for PostgreSQL
(`postgresql+psycopg://...`, see
`src/{{ package_name }}/core/config.py`). The mistake to watch for is
the opposite direction: don't reach for an async driver
(`asyncpg`, `aiosqlite`) or an async engine (`create_async_engine`)
anywhere in this codebase — Flask's request handling is sync end to
end, so an async engine would need an event loop nothing here provides,
not a performance win.

## `:memory:` SQLite needs `StaticPool`

`init_db()` special-cases a `:memory:` database URL: the default
connection pool hands out a *new* connection per checkout, and a
SQLite `:memory:` database only exists for the lifetime of a single
connection — so without `StaticPool` (which keeps one connection alive
for the app's lifetime), every checkout would silently see an empty,
freshly-created database. This only ever triggers for `:memory:` (used
by `tests/conftest.py`'s `create_app(database_url="sqlite:///:memory:")`);
a real file-based SQLite database doesn't need it, which is why it's
gated behind the `:memory:` check rather than applied unconditionally.

## Schema creation and migrations don't coexist

{% if migrations %}
This project has `migrations=true`. `init_db()` only calls
`Base.metadata.create_all(engine)` directly when `testing=True` — real
runs get their schema solely from `flask db upgrade`/`alembic upgrade
head` (see the `alembic` skill). If you're seeing "table does not
exist" errors outside of tests, run the migration — don't remove the
`testing` guard to make `create_all()` run unconditionally, or
`alembic revision --autogenerate` will permanently report "No changes
in schema detected" once the tables already exist without a migration
having created them.
{% else %}
This project has `migrations=false`: `init_db()` always calls
`Base.metadata.create_all(engine)`, so the schema matches the current
models on every app start — convenient, but only correct because
there's no migration history to fall out of sync with. If you later
turn migrations on, this becomes conditional on `testing` (see the
`alembic` skill) — `create_all()` and a real migration history must
never both run against the same non-test database.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
